  "why": "Summary metrics look clean (matching p95, zero errors), but the raw per-route log shows a canary-only tail regression on /search. Facts: 18 of 729 canary /search requests (2.47%) took >=600ms; stable served 2273 /search requests with none above 200ms (max 193ms); canary /search p99 is >=600ms while stable /search p99 is <=100ms even though their p50 and p95 agree within 2ms, which is why the dashboard p95 column hides it; those 18 slow canary requests appear in all four 30s windows of the 120s test (4/4), so this is not a cold-start/warmup artifact; the slow tail is confined to /search (canary has 0 /browse and 1 /checkout request >200ms, vs stable 0 and 7); and neither version returned any 5xx or non-200 response. Judgement: a ~10x p99 tail (652ms vs 61ms) on one route that stable never exhibits and that persists evenly for the whole test is a real regression, not sampling noise - at 2.47% about 1 in 40 searches would stall ~0.65s if the canary took 100% of traffic. Judgement: with zero errors this is latency-only, not an outage, so it is a close call, but a 10x tail on the revenue-adjacent search path is customer-visible and the cause is unexplained (looks like a cache-miss/fallback branch), so the safer call is to roll back, fix the slow path, and re-canary rather than promote.",
